Subject: [PATCH] Keep session-owned CBS uploads safe, observable, and parallel
Base: pinned Cloud Backed SQLite source staged by libdoltlite-sys/build.rs

This is a regenerated net patch from the state after blockcachevfs patches
0001-0016. The consolidation was first generated by applying the former
0017-0025 series in order and proven byte-identical to that result; a later
staging-fault regression exposed an ordinary-upload cursor bug, so this patch
also includes the narrow staged-skip retry fix documented below. During an
upstream refresh, retain each numbered intent and this regression fix; do not
edit the pristine vendored cloudsqlite tree or drop behavior because an older
intermediate patch no longer applies.

Retained intent from the former blockcachevfs patches:
- 0017: Put immutable block payloads under blocks/<hex>.bcv, with no flat-key
  fallback. The explicit blocks/ namespace lets a client CAB authorize immutable
  payload keys without granting session-control objects. Session writes are
  create-only; an existing object is accepted only after exact-byte verification.
  Reject session writes lacking authenticated operation scope so garbage
  collection can protect every client-written block.
- 0018: Fence each attempt-marker write against server cleanup. Read the mutable
  guard ETag before and after publishing the marker, proceed only if both reads
  show the same idle generation (or both show no guard), retry a busy sweep for
  up to 12 seconds, and rotate the idle epoch when cleanup releases its sweep.
- 0019: Send Google JSON CRC32C on media uploads, verify the returned checksum
  before a full-object download reaches CBS consumers, and reject short blocks
  before a cache write. Preserve conditional 304 behavior.
- 0020: For Cargo's bundled static curl only, preserve CLOUDSQLITE_CAINFO, honor
  SSL_CERT_FILE and SSL_CERT_DIR, and otherwise choose readable conventional CA
  paths. Never weaken peer or hostname verification; standalone CBS keeps its
  upstream curl setup.
- 0021: Refresh session-owned Google JSON Bearer credentials at the request
  dispatch boundary and after one 401/403, replaying the same body and
  generation conditions once. Keep static auth and Google XML/S3 behavior, and
  keep token-bearing headers out of verbose logs.
- 0022: Report completed block creation and exact-byte reuse through an
  optional URI-VFS callback; events include streaming staging, checkpoint
  flushes, and ordinary block uploads. The Rust callback is synchronous; panics
  are contained and cannot change storage or publication results.
- 0023: Retain the first terminal storage failure as a safe operation phase and
  explicitly typed HTTP or SQLite cause. Exclude URLs, credentials, provider
  response text, transient retries, missing cleanup guards, expected CAS
  conflicts, and verified immutable-object reuse.
- 0024: After the explicit final checkpoint quiesces the WAL, report the
  remaining unpinned dirty blocks as a fixed upload plan. Plan totals count
  uploaded and verified-reused full block payloads, not network traffic or WAL
  and publication metadata; do not report a plan while production is active or
  dirty blocks are blocked.
- 0025: Stage dirty blocks in bounded parallel batches for watermark and final
  drains. The Rust `upload_concurrency` setting maps to the native
  `SQLITE_BCV_NREQUEST` upper bound. Values from 1 through INT_MAX are accepted;
  1 retains serial staging. Fence markers serially before queueing
  payload PUTs; cap effective concurrency by SQLITE_BCV_NREQUEST, 64 blocks,
  cache capacity, and 64 MiB of
  copied payload (a larger single block stages alone). Drain every request,
  verify create-only conflicts by exact GET, durably retain successful siblings
  independently, and keep completed sibling progress visible when another PUT
  fails. Measure the watermark from dirty payload blocks so later writes keep
  forming batches.

Follow-up correctness fixes after the original consolidation equivalence
proof:
- When ordinary final upload skips a block already durably staged, it must
  continue scanning that request chain. Without this retry, each initial
  request chain may stop at its first staged block and leave later dirty
  blocks out of the object store even though the manifest includes them. The
  `bcvfs_staging_fault.test` regression checks a staged prefix longer than the
  configured upload concurrency followed by a dirty tail, then verifies a cold
  read.
- Ordinary, unscoped writers retain the shared writer-guard CAS once per batch
  before any block payload PUT. If cleanup owns SWEEP, no payload from that
  batch is queued and dirty local blocks remain retryable. Session-owned CABs
  cannot mutate the shared guard and continue using only the read-idle,
  attempt-marker, read-epoch fence. Keep the zero-PUT-under-SWEEP and
  idle-transition recovery checks in `bcvfs_session_gc.test`.

Cross-tree coordination: the Rust integration pairs this CBS physical
block-upload/reuse callback with DoltLite patch 0009's separate logical chunk
plan and progress hook. The cloud Bearer refresh API is registered by
rusqdoltlite for session-owned GCS URIs. These are separate native interfaces;
standalone CBS builds do not require DoltLite. Keep CBS and DoltLite source
edits in their respective patch series. build.rs applies this patch only to an
OUT_DIR staging copy.
